{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact head 6e585cf99cf76bf377f46dc114a3f8e16f616f91, implementation request df6ff8d361dc8940e66f9eb59d8cad91d001ffca, review invitation 8f85630b2484af3e3a6f1b23a315969df02d75f1. I inspected all nine effective artifacts, each bound directly to builder promise 60592a5bfcdc635577e721436116a67c88608987 at this head, with plans/README.md primary. No blocking finding in this delivery.\n\nThe reported failure has a concrete causal path: propose.pinObjects routes the canonical mint through MintLedger; the provider's reported expiry exceeded the Room's arrival plus requested TTL by 41–67 ms, so the exact comparison refused the token before admission. The same check affected publication and other canonical mints. The fix explicitly changes R-MINT-3 and its design note to allow 5,000 ms on that generic comparison only. It retains the absolute notAfter bound and the known-ID revocation duty for rejected answers.\n\nIndependent validation in disposable clones: root npm test passed 2,375 tests with one skipped; root typecheck, Git Workers (11 tests), Git test:log (12 tests) and the Room local deployment dry run with locked Wrangler 4.146.0 all passed. On exact pre-fix 965c911a58687f634a03a820826c0fd619246185, copying only the new Room regression yielded a runtime failure at its first propose for 67 ms skew, while the excessive-expiry control passed. On the candidate, both Room tests passed, including two admitted proposals, a second landing through a merge, publication and no remaining canonical tokens or ledger duties. Four separate source mutations were caught at runtime: remove the allowance, remove the generic bound, make the allowance boundary exclusive, and extend notAfter by the allowance. The first two also failed the composed Room tests on the corresponding success/refusal assertions. Restoring the source passed all 48 MintLedger tests and both Room regressions. No compile or setup failure is counted as a killed mutant.\n\nArchitecture: accepted. Cross-service clock comparison is handled at the ledger's answer-classification boundary, shared by canonical callers. No application-specific workaround, new authority path, persistence schema or migration is introduced. The protocol states the changed contract, rather than letting an implementation tolerance silently redefine it. The exported constant supports consistent future composition; the separate fork ledger must align with this allowance under its existing changes-requested review 865ef31c, not duplicate the obsolete exact comparison.\n\nSecurity: accepted for this change. The requested scope, readable expiry, waiting-caller checks, known-ID ownership and absolute job deadline remain enforced. A reply exactly 5,000 ms beyond the generic bound passes; 5,001 ms and notAfter+1 do not. This is a bounded acceptance tolerance, not a claim that clock skew is guaranteed below 5 seconds, not an extension of an absolute deadline, and not permission to settle unknown create outcomes by elapsed request time. The temporary diagnosis-in-response builds described in the report are absent from this candidate. The separate received-ID persistence and fork sweep repairs remain under their existing request and review; this approval does not close them.\n\nSimplification and broader value: accepted. One comparison and a named constant restore the canonical propose, landing and publication paths without adding retries or per-caller exceptions. Runtime and storage complexity are unchanged. The report explains why the shared-clock test world missed the defect and adds a regression with separate clocks; that is useful coverage for future provider integration. The observed tail delay and alarm-event attribution are documented, with their runtime cause expressly unresolved.\n\nLive evidence and limits: I inspected the committed 03:35:10 smoke record: 91/91 steps, four admitted proposals and successful landings, public/import/checks log verification through 14/8/15, and cleanup with no unresolved duties or repositories left. That is builder-run evidence for Room 6d15d828 / checkers c18342a3 built from 0753d7de, not my own deployment or a live run of the later merged head. The report's opening status correctly discloses that the spike lacks the merged row-write changes; read the table's “source as at this head” as the clock fix, not the whole application. The clock code and contract are unchanged between that deployed revision and the candidate. Local gates cover the merged candidate. The log verifier's stated proof limits remain; this smoke does not establish full state-transition replay or a new cost measurement.\n\nGit provenance: the three implementing/report/merge commits carry the full request Rests-On trailer. diff --check passes, and merge-tree against main b44601dd1015259f9d4a7a6320148500194605e8 is clean (tree 189590e1e75a965d429bccd5a6133331629bddc1). This verdict approves only the named candidate and its declared protocol allowance; landing requires the normal gitseq authorization and receipt.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:14fe25580fc2afac09d061d10f2d31a086d91759","binding":"assigned","head":"6e585cf99cf76bf377f46dc114a3f8e16f616f91","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2aaae267c0b9860955b2e95740e26daeb358d1d8\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:60592a5bfcdc635577e721436116a67c88608987\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:80d2c405c4d34abbbb52bed9266dbe6d26e45f0e","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}